WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:05.760
Thanks for having me and for the opportunity to speak on this

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.470
wonderful topic of our security.

3
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.230
Let me start with something personal.

4
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.110
This morning I was a little bit disappointed.

5
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:22.600
I woke up early. I saw the weather outside and the sun is

6
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:29.680
shining, the weather in the bay was so crystal clear

7
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:35.480
and calm, and I was disappointed not because I missed the German

8
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:41.120
weather that I got used to over the past 23 years living there,

9
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:47.560
but because the nice weather outside is not going to support very

10
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:54.030
much my keynote. That's why I would like to ask you to

11
00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.120
use your imagination, close your eyes briefly,

12
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:04.080
and imagine that you're a captain of a vessel and you're crossing

13
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.430
the Black Sea. And

14
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.110
it is not a pleasant trip.

15
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.120
You're responsible for the crew, you're responsible for the

16
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.350
boat, you're responsible for the mission.

17
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.950
And feel free to open your eyes, of course,

18
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.190
not to miss any of the wonderful slides.

19
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:31.590
And suddenly the weather changes

20
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.560
not according to the forecast, not like yesterday.

21
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:39.200
I was watching the weather forecast for Varna.

22
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.480
It was saying cloudy. And yeah, this morning I saw some clouds in

23
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:50.320
the sky, but not sufficient to support this message,

24
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:56.470
right? So the weather changes, the GPS

25
00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.910
becomes unreliable,

26
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:05.150
and on the horizon there are unidentified vessels.

27
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:11.320
During the journey, you receive new regulations that you need to

28
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:15.120
comply with. You need to have safety equipment on board that you're

29
00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:19.030
not carrying. And on top of this,

30
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:25.400
half of your crew have started using AI navigation tools that

31
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.800
you haven't approved, that you're not controlling,

32
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:31.190
that you don't govern at all.

33
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.800
So, ladies and gentlemen, in such a moment, what would be your

34
00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.950
approach? Are you going to purchase a new radar system?

35
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.640
Of course not. Rather, you're going to start to redesign

36
00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.350
the boat and the processes on the boat.

37
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.920
And basically, ladies and gentlemen, this is the situation in

38
00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.190
which every organization in this room, public or private,

39
00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.710
is currently in.

40
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:07.590
Welcome

41
00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.230
to the permanent storm and the perfect storm.

42
00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:17.720
The metaphor is not decorative only, because many of the

43
00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:21.270
parallels apply to the organizational context as well.

44
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.840
At the end of the day, it is not the best equipped ship that is

45
00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:32.840
surviving the storm, but rather the one that is built in

46
00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:37.200
a resilient way, and is prepared to balance the load,

47
00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:44.080
to contain failures, and to basically keep sailing

48
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.150
under pressure. So,

49
00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.350
why do we have the perfect storm?

50
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:57.320
There are five forces that are converging simultaneously without

51
00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:03.150
waiting for each other. On one side we have the

52
00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:09.440
geopolitical situation that is changing the way we do

53
00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:12.190
business continuity planning and capacity planning.

54
00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:16.640
We have state actors that are attacking critical

55
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.349
infrastructure like energy infrastructure,

56
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.230
logistics, and health care.

57
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.469
So, the weather doesn't wait for us to prepare.

58
00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.040
It just hits us, right? On the other side we have artificial

59
00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:37.320
intelligence as a topic.

60
00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.990
This is one of the most significant force multipliers

61
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:44.190
that are actively

62
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.870
influencing both the attacker and the defender side.

63
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.919
The bad thing is it's not in a balanced way.

64
00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:59.400
The attackers are much faster in adopting AI simply because

65
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.040
they don't have the corporate processes, they don't have a

66
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.160
purchasing process, they don't have a change advisory board

67
00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:13.240
and so on and so forth. So, we don't have an equal start here with

68
00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:17.080
them. On the other side the question is not anymore if the

69
00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:22.030
organization we are responsible for is consuming AI.

70
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.040
The question is where we use it and do we have an appropriate

71
00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:28.710
governance on top of it?

72
00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:35.919
Regulations like NIS 2, DORA, Cyber Resilience Act,

73
00:05:35.919 --> 00:05:39.760
and so on. They're not technical frameworks anymore.

74
00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.430
They're creating the necessity for governance and they're putting

75
00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:49.080
liability consequences, personal liabilities, on the senior

76
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:54.680
management. At the same time, classical threats like malware,

77
00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.310
ransomware, they haven't disappeared.

78
00:05:58.120 --> 00:05:59.040
So, they're still there.

79
00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.040
They have become more professionalized and with the use of AI,

80
00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:07.920
of course, the impact and the speed of attack is much, much

81
00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.430
higher. And last but not least, your crew.

82
00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.430
They adopt AI technologies that are not following the processes.

83
00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.350
They are not well trained on them.

84
00:06:21.919 --> 00:06:28.390
And you basically have a much broader shadow IT to deal with.

85
00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.280
So, the first instinct in such a situation is, if you have a

86
00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:38.280
problem, we search for a solution, right?

87
00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.320
So, if you have a new ransomware attack, then we look for a better

88
00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:46.120
endpoint detection. Or if we have a new regulation,

89
00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:47.960
we look for a compliance tool.

90
00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:53.400
Or if we have AI threats to deal with, then we're looking for

91
00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.670
an AI security product. Well, this is understandable,

92
00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:01.430
but at the same time, all of us know that it's insufficient.

93
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.520
It is kind of like optimizing the components of a system without

94
00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.790
recognizing that the system is fragile.

95
00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.350
So, it is important to understand that

96
00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.480
the ship that survives is not the one that

97
00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:29.590
is best equipped, but

98
00:07:30.919 --> 00:07:33.710
the one that has an appropriate architecture.

99
00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:37.720
If you look at the breaches of the recent years,

100
00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:42.480
all these big organizations with big IT budgets and cybersecurity

101
00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:47.030
budgets, they had tons of security tools in place.

102
00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.560
What they didn't have is probably an appropriate cybersecurity

103
00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.150
architecture strategy.

104
00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.590
So, what does, at the end of the day, business-oriented

105
00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.950
cybersecurity architecture mean?

106
00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:10.240
Because this is the proposed solution, right?

107
00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:14.120
Well, it is a combination of seven principles,

108
00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:16.830
or it can be defined as a combination of seven principles.

109
00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.120
Some of the most important ones are: put business first.

110
00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:26.030
So, when we build a strategy, when we define a strategy,

111
00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:30.840
the important point is to understand what is the core of our

112
00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:36.000
business. What are the crucial processes that we have that we need to

113
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.000
protect, the assets, the processes, the systems around them,

114
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.160
the roles in the organization without which

115
00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:45.830
our business is not going to function.

116
00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:49.950
And we need to align our strategy with these needs.

117
00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:51.990
The second, governance.

118
00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:56.350
We need to clearly define who owns the risk,

119
00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:01.920
who is capable of approving decisions, who is responsible for

120
00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.880
reacting in certain situations.

121
00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:08.360
That's why regulations like NIS 2 and DORA

122
00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:12.640
are not putting requirements on new security tools.

123
00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:16.270
They are defining the necessity of better governance.

124
00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:23.800
Identity. It is the new perimeter.

125
00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.310
You've heard this buzzword, right?

126
00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:30.640
But I mean, imagine if someone is on the ship,

127
00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.790
we cannot automatically start to trust them

128
00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.990
because we don't know what their motivations are.

129
00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:42.600
We don't know what, intentionally or unintentionally, the

130
00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:49.480
people are up to. So, identity is a very significant

131
00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:54.720
principle that we need to cover, and how we cover this is

132
00:09:54.720 --> 00:10:00.240
by verifying the identity continuously, and not only at the login

133
00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.430
but during the lifetime of the usage.

134
00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.360
One of the most important principles from my perspective is

135
00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:11.470
continuous adaptation.

136
00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:15.910
An architecture is not a static thing.

137
00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.320
Architecture lives. It needs to be adapted.

138
00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:20.510
It needs to be reassessed.

139
00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:24.830
You need to observe what the changes in the threat landscape are,

140
00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:27.360
what the changes in the business context are,

141
00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.440
what the changes in the business needs in general are.

142
00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:34.360
Are there technology changes that are affecting our security

143
00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:39.160
strategies? So, architecture needs to be adapted and needs to be

144
00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:44.680
revisited often. And last but not least from the principles is

145
00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:49.190
the risk-driven design. This is my favorite one.

146
00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.400
I mean, if you ask a security professional what is the goal of

147
00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:57.000
cybersecurity, many people tend to say, "Well,

148
00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.550
to have the best possible security controls in our organization."

149
00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:04.150
And honestly, I believe this is a mistake.

150
00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:10.720
Because none of the organizations of this world aim to be secure

151
00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:12.480
for the sake of being secure.

152
00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:16.960
They are there to do business and to operate in a

153
00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:22.680
resilient way. So, it is always a matter of balancing between the

154
00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.480
business needs, the business-relevant threats,

155
00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.600
the risk associated with them, and the cost of the controls that

156
00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:35.120
you're applying in order to achieve an optimal return of risk

157
00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:36.390
reduction and investment

158
00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:41.270
according to your own risk appetite.

159
00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:48.230
Coming to the end of my keynote

160
00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:54.750
talk, well, ladies and gentlemen, we cannot calm the sea

161
00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:02.720
and obviously we cannot stop the storm, and I mean, obviously

162
00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:07.680
no regulations are going to stop any geopolitical conflict, and no

163
00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:10.230
tool or AI will stop the uncertainty

164
00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:16.800
that is appearing, nor are tools going to fix human errors, but

165
00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:21.870
the matter is what type of ship are we building?

166
00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:28.040
And if I need to give you three recommendations today that will

167
00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.470
stay with you after this conversation,

168
00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:38.240
well, stop asking yourself if we have the right tools; start to ask

169
00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:39.630
if you have the right architecture.

170
00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:45.200
Stop asking yourself if your IT department is covering this or that gap

171
00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:48.839
but start to ask yourself if your leadership is making the right

172
00:12:48.839 --> 00:12:53.839
decisions. And last but not least, focus not on compliance but on

173
00:12:53.839 --> 00:12:57.990
resilience, because compliance will naturally come at the end.

174
00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:00.710
Thank you very much for your attention.
